from common import lesson, lab, cite

LESSONS = [
lesson(24,6,'correlation-and-screening','Correlation, permutation, and thousands of candidates',
 'Measure how two columns move together, test it by shuffling, and count how many candidates would pass by chance alone.',
 '''<h2 id="two-numbers">Two numbers for one question</h2>
<p>A new kind of eval question appears when you look inside a model. You have a candidate unit: a neuron, a direction in activation space, or a feature from a dictionary. You compute its activation on many inputs. You also have a label for each input, such as whether it mentions a date, or a predicted activation written down from an explanation. The question is whether the activation column and the label column move together, and whether that co-movement is more than noise.</p>
<p>The Pearson correlation answers the first part on a linear scale. For n paired observations (x₁,y₁)…(xₙ,yₙ) with means x̄ and ȳ:</p>
<div class="math">r = Σ(xᵢ−x̄)(yᵢ−ȳ) / √[Σ(xᵢ−x̄)² · Σ(yᵢ−ȳ)²]</div>
<p>Each term in the numerator is a product of two deviations: positive when both values sit on the same side of their means, negative when they sit on opposite sides. The denominator scales the sum so the result has no units and lies between −1 and +1. Multiplying every activation by ten, or adding a constant, leaves r unchanged. Zero means no linear co-variation, which is weaker than independence: a U-shaped relation can have r near zero. '''+cite('pearson')+'''</p>
<p>The Spearman correlation replaces each column by its ranks (1 for the smallest value, n for the largest, ties sharing the average of the ranks they occupy) and then computes Pearson’s r on the ranks. It measures any monotone relation and ignores how far apart the values are, so one extreme activation cannot dominate it. When there are no ties, a shortcut gives the same number from the rank differences dᵢ = rank(xᵢ) − rank(yᵢ): ρ = 1 − 6Σdᵢ² / [n(n²−1)]. '''+cite('spearman')+'''</p>
<h2 id="pencil">A pencil example</h2>
<p>Eight inputs, with a candidate’s activation x and a label y that is a continuous feature of the input. The eighth input is unusual: its label is 20 while the others stay below 8.</p>
<table><tr><th>i</th><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>x−x̄</th><th>y−ȳ</th><th>product</th><th>rank x</th><th>rank y</th><th>d</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>−3.5</td><td>−3</td><td>10.5</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>−2</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>−2.5</td><td>−5</td><td>12.5</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>−1.5</td><td>−4</td><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>−0.5</td><td>−1</td><td>0.5</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>−1</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>4</td><td>0.5</td><td>−2</td><td>−1</td><td>5</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>1.5</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>2.5</td><td>1</td><td>2.5</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>20</td><td>3.5</td><td>14</td><td>49</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
<p>The means are x̄ = 36/8 = 4.5 and ȳ = 48/8 = 6. The products sum to 80. The squared x deviations sum to 42 and the squared y deviations to 252, so r = 80/√(42 × 252) = 80/√10,584 ≈ 80/102.9 ≈ 0.778. The single product of 49 from the eighth input contributes more than half of the numerator.</p>
<p>For Spearman, y has no ties, so the shortcut applies. The squared rank differences sum to 4 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 8, giving ρ = 1 − 48/(8 × 63) = 1 − 48/504 ≈ 0.905. The ranks do not care that the last label is 20 rather than 8. Both numbers are correct; they answer different questions. Report which one you used and why.</p>
<h2 id="permutation">Shuffle the labels: the permutation test</h2>
<p>Is r = 0.778 more than eight random pairs would produce? One null hypothesis says the labels carry no information about the activations: any way of attaching the eight labels to the eight activations was equally likely. Under that null you can generate the whole null distribution yourself. Keep the activations in place, reorder the labels, recompute r, and repeat.</p>
<p>With n = 8 there are 8! = 40,320 orderings, few enough to enumerate every one. The two-sided permutation p-value is the fraction of orderings whose |r| is at least the observed 0.778. The lab command below enumerates them: for Pearson the fraction is 0.0030; for Spearman 0.0046. The exact meaning is modest and precise: if the labels were exchangeable, about three orderings in a thousand would show a linear co-variation this strong. It does not say the probability that the association is real, and it says nothing about how large the association is. '''+cite('permutation')+'''</p>
<p>For larger n, enumeration is impossible and the lab draws random orderings instead. With K random relabelings and c of them at least as extreme, it reports p = (c + 1)/(K + 1), counting the observed ordering as one draw; this keeps the smallest reportable p at 1/(K + 1) rather than zero. The test needs no normality assumption, which is why it suits activations, which are usually skewed with a long upper tail. For comparison, the normal-theory p-value for the same eight pairs is 0.0231, nearly eight times larger, because the formula does not know that the single large label is one point rather than a normal tail.</p>
<p>The permutation test does assume that the observations are exchangeable under the null. Tokens from the same document, or inputs that share a template, are not. If the dependence matters, permute at the level of the independent unit, as <a href="11-clusters-and-repeats.html">lesson 11</a> did for repositories: shuffle labels between documents, not between tokens within a document.</p>
<h2 id="thousands">Thousands of candidates</h2>
<p>A model has thousands of neurons, and a screen tests every one against the same label. Suppose 3,000 candidates and none of them has any real association. At a threshold of p < 0.05, each one has a 5% chance of passing by chance, so the expected count of false positives is 3,000 × 0.05 = 150. The lab simulation with seed seven produces 151. Any report that announces “we found 150 label-sensitive neurons” from such a screen has found exactly what noise promises.</p>
<p>Two corrections take different goals. Bonferroni tests each candidate at α/m, here 0.05/3,000 ≈ 0.0000167, and bounds the probability of even one false rejection across the family. '''+cite('bonferroni')+''' Benjamini–Hochberg controls the false discovery rate: the expected fraction of rejections that are false. Sort the m p-values, find the largest i for which p₍ᵢ₎ ≤ (i/m)·q, and reject the i smallest. The procedure was introduced by Benjamini and Hochberg in 1995 and is valid for independent tests or positively dependent ones. '''+cite('bh1995')+''' '''+cite('fdr-scipy')+'''</p>
<table><tr><th>rank i</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th></tr>
<tr><td>sorted p</td><td>0.0004</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.009</td><td>0.02</td><td>0.03</td><td>0.06</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.8</td></tr>
<tr><td>(i/8) × 0.05</td><td>0.00625</td><td>0.0125</td><td>0.01875</td><td>0.025</td><td>0.03125</td><td>0.0375</td><td>0.04375</td><td>0.05</td></tr>
<tr><td>p ≤ threshold?</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
<p>The largest i that passes is 5, so Benjamini–Hochberg rejects the five smallest p-values at q = 0.05. Bonferroni at 0.05/8 = 0.00625 rejects only the first two. Note the step-up logic: a p-value can be rejected even if it fails its own threshold, as long as a larger one further down the list passes.</p>
<p>In the all-null simulation both rules reject nothing. Now plant thirty candidates with a real association strong enough to matter (<code>--real 30 --effect 1</code>). In that run, 178 candidates pass the raw threshold, of which 148 are noise; Bonferroni keeps 25 of the thirty real ones and no noise; Benjamini–Hochberg keeps 29 and no noise. The generator knows which were planted, so the lab can print the realized false-discovery count. A real screen cannot, which is why the guarantee is an expectation over screens, not a certificate for one. '''+cite('fdr')+'''</p>
<p>Neurons in one layer are not independent tests: they co-activate on the same inputs. Positive dependence is covered by the Benjamini–Hochberg guarantee; arbitrary dependence is not, and the Benjamini–Yekutieli variant or a permutation of the whole screen are the usual responses. Match the dependence structure before trusting a count.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>State the family before screening: how many candidates, which label, which statistic, which rule. “3,000 neurons, Spearman against the date label, Benjamini–Hochberg at q = 0.05, labels permuted within documents” is a reviewable procedure. A list of the 40 highest correlations is a search result.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading a tiny permutation p-value as a large effect, or as the probability the association is real. With 50,000 tokens, r = 0.04 can have p below 0.0001 and still explain almost nothing about what the unit does.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague screens 2,000 features against a label, keeps the 95 with p < 0.05, and calls them the label’s feature set. Before looking at any of them, what do you expect that list to contain?',
 'Under the null alone, 2,000 × 0.05 = 100 features would pass. Ninety-five is what pure noise delivers, so the list is uninformative as it stands. Apply a family-level rule, then confirm survivors on data not used for the screen. If Benjamini–Hochberg rejects nothing, the honest report is that the screen found no feature distinguishable from noise at that sample size.',
 lab(['python3 labs/stats_lab.py correlate --x "1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8" --y "3 1 2 5 4 6 7 20"','python3 labs/stats_lab.py screen','python3 labs/stats_lab.py screen --real 30 --effect 1'],
 'Recompute the first command’s Pearson and Spearman values by hand from the table before running it. Then predict the raw count in the all-null screen, and predict whether Bonferroni or Benjamini–Hochberg will keep more of the thirty planted candidates.',
 'Your pencil values should match 0.7776 and 0.9048. The all-null screen passes about 150 of 3,000 at p < 0.05 and both corrections reject nothing. With planted signal, Benjamini–Hochberg keeps more real candidates than Bonferroni, because it bounds a fraction rather than any single error. Seeds other than seven change the exact counts; the expectation does not.'), ['pearson','spearman','permutation','bh1995','fdr-scipy','bonferroni','fdr']),

lesson(25,6,'confirm-on-held-out-data','Confirm a screened finding on held-out data',
 'The best candidate from a screen was chosen because it looked good. Only data it has never touched can say how good it is.',
 '''<h2 id="winner">The best of many is not a typical one</h2>
<p>Return to the all-null screen from <a href="24-correlation-and-screening.html">lesson 24</a>: 3,000 candidates, fifty observations each, no real association anywhere. The largest |r| in that run was 0.471. That is not a bug in the simulation. Each candidate’s r is a draw from a null distribution centered at zero; take the maximum of 3,000 draws and you get a value far out in the tail. If you had been handed that candidate alone, with its p-value of 0.00056, it would have looked like a discovery.</p>
<p>The same logic applies when real associations exist. Write any candidate’s observed correlation as truth plus noise. Selecting the maximum prefers candidates whose noise happened to be positive, so the winner’s observed value overstates its truth. This is the winner’s curse, a form of selection bias. The upward bias is largest when candidates are many, samples are small, and true effects are weak, which describes a neuron screen exactly. The p-value from the screening data inherits the same problem: it was computed as if this candidate were the only one tested.</p>
<h2 id="split">Split before you look</h2>
<p>The fix is to keep the data used for choosing separate from the data used for scoring. Divide the observations into a screening half and a held-out half before computing anything. Screen on the first half, pick the winner, then compute its correlation on the second half. The held-out value has no selection in it: the winner was chosen without seeing those observations, so its held-out r is an ordinary estimate with ordinary uncertainty.</p>
<p>The lab’s <code>screen</code> command does this split. In the all-null run, the winner on the screening half had r = −0.630 on twenty-five observations and r = −0.098 on the held-out twenty-five: the entire effect was selection. With thirty planted candidates at effect one, the screening winner showed r = 0.777 and held out at 0.739: a real association survives, a little smaller, as expected. On the course fixture of sixty candidates, the winner <code>unit-003</code> went from 0.794 on its first twenty observations to 0.650 on its last twenty.</p>
<p>A held-out set stays unbiased only while it stays unused. The moment you look at held-out scores, adjust the candidate, and look again, the held-out set has become screening data. The scikit-learn guide describes the same hazard for model tuning: knowledge of the test set leaks into the choices and the score stops reporting generalization. '''+cite('holdout')+''' When a finding matters, three sets are the clean design: screen, confirm, and a final set touched once. A fresh sample collected after the claim is fixed is better still.</p>
<p>Splitting shares the earlier lessons’ requirement to respect structure. Split by document, repository, or prompt family, not by token or attempt. Tokens from the same document on both sides of the split leak the screening data into the confirmation.</p>
<h2 id="prediction">Write the prediction before the look</h2>
<p>A confirmation is a prediction test. Before scoring on the held-out data, write down four things: which candidate, which direction and rough size the association should have, which held-out observations will be used, and what score would count as failure. A prediction that cannot fail is not a test. “Unit 003’s activation will correlate positively with the date label on the held-out documents, with Spearman ρ above 0.3; below 0.1 we drop the claim” can be checked by anyone with the file.</p>
<p>This does not forbid exploration. Screens are how candidates are found, and looking at the top activations is how explanations are written. The discipline is to label the exploration as exploration, record how many candidates were tried, and give the claim its own untouched data. <a href="17-multiple-comparisons.html">Lesson 17</a> made the same point for prompt tournaments; a neuron screen is a tournament with thousands of entrants.</p>
<h2 id="controls">Controls that would also pass if the claim were empty</h2>
<p>A held-out correlation of 0.65 is only impressive relative to what an empty claim would score. Run at least one control that should fail if the claim has content.</p>
<table><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th><th>What it rules out</th></tr>
<tr><td>Random candidate</td><td>Score several unselected units on the same held-out data</td><td>Held-out data that correlate with everything, for example because the label tracks input length</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shuffled labels</td><td>Permute the held-out labels across independent units and rescore</td><td>A scoring pipeline that produces high values regardless of alignment; also gives the held-out permutation p-value</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trivial-feature baseline</td><td>Predict the activation from something simple, such as token frequency, position, or the label alone</td><td>A specific explanation that adds nothing beyond a generic feature</td></tr></table>
<p>The random-candidate control calibrates the scale: if ordinary units score 0.3 on this label, 0.65 is strong; if they score 0.6, the label is nearly uninformative about which unit you picked. The shuffled-labels control is the permutation test of lesson 24 applied on the confirmation side. The trivial baseline asks whether your explanation beats a dumb one. An explanation that scores 0.65 when “fires on long inputs” scores 0.62 has not yet earned its wording.</p>
<h2 id="explanations">Scoring an explanation of a neuron</h2>
<p>The same machinery scores natural-language explanations of what a unit does. Turn the explanation into a prediction: for each token of a text, how strongly should the unit fire? That predicted column, whether written by hand or produced by a model acting as a simulator, is a candidate label. Its correlation with the real activations is the explanation’s score. The released scoring code from an automated-explanation project computes exactly this: the Pearson correlation between real and predicted activations, pooled across all scored text rather than averaged per sequence. '''+cite('neuron-explainer')+'''</p>
<p>Everything in this module applies. The explanation was written from the unit’s top-activating examples, so scoring it on those same examples is scoring on screening data. Score on held-out text. Thousands of explanations, one per unit, form a family. Pearson ignores scale, so a simulator that predicts a tenth of the true activation gets the same r as one that predicts it exactly; pair r with a check on magnitude, such as explained variance, when scale matters. And run the controls: an explanation that scores no better than a shuffled one, or than “fires on any capitalized word,” has not been demonstrated.</p>
<p>The rule this course has used since <a href="01-quantities-and-denominators.html">lesson 01</a> holds for a claim about a model’s internals: name the quantity, name the data it will be tested on, name the control, then look. <a href="../../../evals/evals-tut/index.html">Understanding LLM Evals</a> treats the measurement side, including how a grader or a simulator is itself validated on held-out cases. The forthcoming <a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">Interpretability Methods and Rigor</a> course applies the prediction test to real units in a small language model.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In eval and interpretability work</span><p>Report a screened finding as three numbers: the screening correlation, the held-out correlation, and the control’s held-out correlation. If only the first exists, the finding is a hypothesis. A reviewer should be able to ask “what did a random unit score?” and get an answer from the same table.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reusing the held-out set. After the third round of “adjust the explanation, rescore on held-out text,” the held-out score is a development score with a misleading name. Count the rounds, and keep a final set that is scored once.</p></div>''',
 'A screen of 4,000 units finds one with r = 0.52 against a label. On a held-out split it scores 0.49, and ten random units score between −0.08 and 0.11 on the same split. A trivial baseline that predicts activation from token position scores 0.45. What has been shown?',
 'The association is real and survives selection: 0.49 on held-out data against a random-unit range near zero. But the specific claim about the label is weak, because position alone reaches 0.45. The unit may respond to position, and the label may correlate with position. The next step is to test the label after controlling for position, or on held-out text where the two are decoupled.',
 lab(['python3 labs/stats_lab.py screen --data labs/data/candidates.jsonl','python3 labs/stats_lab.py correlate --candidate unit-003','python3 labs/stats_lab.py correlate --candidate unit-000'],
 'Before running, predict how many of the sixty fixture candidates pass p < 0.05 by chance, then compare the raw, Bonferroni, and Benjamini–Hochberg counts. Treat <code>unit-000</code> as your random-candidate control for <code>unit-003</code>.',
 'Three of sixty are expected by chance; the run shows ten raw passes, four of them unplanted. Benjamini–Hochberg keeps five, all planted; Bonferroni keeps none, because sixty candidates at forty observations leave little room under 0.05/60. The control unit’s correlation and its permutation p-value show what an empty claim scores on the same label; the planted unit’s held-out value is the number to report, not its screening value.'), ['holdout','neuron-explainer','permutation','fdr-scipy','asa']),
]
